Description: CVE-2026-53791 - daemon argument-list bound + proxy-protocol peer/length hardening
 Backport of the upstream fix (rsync 3.2.7 line) to rsync 3.2.3 (Debian 11).
 The 3.2.3 daemon lacks the proxy_protocol_hosts trusted-peer plumbing, so the
 loadparm parameter, the access.c matcher, and the clientserver.c gating are
 added alongside the upstream length/argument-count hardening.
 .
 The patch:
  * io.c: bounds a malicious daemon client's argument list so argv cannot be
    grown without limit (memory-exhaustion DoS).
  * socket.c: bounds the outgoing PROXY CONNECT request and the incoming proxy
    response header lines, fixing an off-by-one stack out-of-bounds write when a
    hostile/MITM proxy returns an over-long, newline-less response line.
  * access.c / daemon-parm.txt / clientserver.c: adds a new global
    "proxy protocol hosts" parameter and requires the direct socket peer to
    match it before an inbound "proxy protocol = true" header is trusted
    (reject untrusted proxy peers, fail-closed); warns at startup when the
    trusted-proxy list is unset so the fail-closed behaviour is not silent.
 .
 A regression test (testsuite/proxy-response-line-too-long.test) exercises the
 over-long proxy response line and asserts rsync rejects it cleanly without
 crashing.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/RsyncProject/rsync/commit/e9b22cd40446bb34d005ecb44519e11795d4be53
