+++ b/testsuite/daemon-auth-users-comma-only.test
@@ -0,0 +1,106 @@
+#!/bin/sh
+
+# Regression test for CVE-2026-70463: a leading comma in the daemon
+# "auth users" setting must make splitting happen on commas ALONE, so that an
+# entry may legitimately contain spaces.  auth_server() used to tokenise on
+# commas AND whitespace (strtok(users, " ,\t")), which tore a spaced entry in
+# two: the rule the administrator wrote never matched, and a rule they never
+# wrote appeared out of its tail -- an authorization bypass (CWE-863).
+#
+# This test drives the "spaced" direction, which needs no NSS group whose name
+# contains a space (a test cannot create one):
+#
+#     auth users = ,@nosuchgroup authuser:deny, authuser:rw
+#
+#   * parsed as documented -- one entry "@nosuchgroup authuser:deny" naming a
+#     group that does not exist, so no match, then "authuser:rw" grants access.
+#   * parsed by the buggy whitespace split -- "@nosuchgroup", then
+#     "authuser:deny", which matches the username and refuses the transfer that
+#     should succeed.
+#
+# A "plain" control module (auth users = authuser:rw) proves the credential and
+# transfer machinery themselves are sound, so a "spaced" failure can only be the
+# parsing defect.
+
+. "$suitedir/rsync.fns"
+
+# rsync must be built with an md-digest daemon-auth (SHA) or plain auth support;
+# every supported build has this, but guard just in case the helper is missing.
+case "x$RSYNC" in
+x) test_skipped "RSYNC is not set" ;;
+esac
+
+USER=authuser
+PASSWORD=known-password
+DATA='AUTH-USERS-PARSE'
+
+conf="$scratchdir/comma-only-rsyncd.conf"
+pidfile="$scratchdir/comma-only-rsyncd.pid"
+logfile="$scratchdir/comma-only-rsyncd.log"
+secrets="$scratchdir/comma-only.secrets"
+pwfile="$scratchdir/comma-only.password"
+
+srcdir2="$scratchdir/src"
+spaceddir="$scratchdir/spaced"
+plaindir="$scratchdir/plain"
+makepath "$srcdir2" "$spaceddir" "$plaindir"
+echo "$DATA" >"$srcdir2/f1"
+
+echo "$USER:$PASSWORD" >"$secrets"
+chmod 600 "$secrets"
+echo "$PASSWORD" >"$pwfile"
+chmod 600 "$pwfile"
+
+my_uid=`get_testuid`
+root_uid=`get_rootuid`
+root_gid=`get_rootgid`
+uid_setting="uid = $root_uid"
+gid_setting="gid = $root_gid"
+if test x"$my_uid" != x"$root_uid"; then
+    uid_setting="#$uid_setting"
+    gid_setting="#$gid_setting"
+fi
+
+cat >"$conf" <<EOF
+pid file = $pidfile
+use chroot = no
+log file = $logfile
+$uid_setting
+$gid_setting
+
+[plain]
+	path = $plaindir
+	read only = no
+	auth users = $USER:rw
+	secrets file = $secrets
+
+[spaced]
+	path = $spaceddir
+	read only = no
+	auth users = ,@nosuchgroup $USER:deny, $USER:rw
+	secrets file = $secrets
+EOF
+
+RSYNC_CONNECT_PROG="$RSYNC --config=$conf --daemon"
+export RSYNC_CONNECT_PROG
+
+# Positive control: a plain "auth users" must admit the credential and land the
+# file.  If this fails, nothing below is meaningful.
+$RSYNC -r "--password-file=$pwfile" "$srcdir2/" "$USER@localhost::plain/" 2>&1 | tee "$scratchdir/out.plain"
+if test ! -f "$plaindir/f1"; then
+    test_fail "positive control failed: the credential could not push to a module with a plain \"auth users\""
+fi
+
+# The fix: a comma-led "auth users" whose first entry names a nonexistent group
+# and contains a space must be parsed comma-only, so "$USER:rw" grants access.
+# The pre-fix whitespace split reads "$USER:deny" out of the tail and refuses.
+$RSYNC -r "--password-file=$pwfile" "$srcdir2/" "$USER@localhost::spaced/" 2>&1 | tee "$scratchdir/out.spaced"
+if test ! -f "$spaceddir/f1"; then
+    test_fail "\"auth users\" starting with a comma was split on whitespace: the entry \"@nosuchgroup $USER:deny\" names a group that does not exist and must not match, but its tail was read as a separate \"$USER:deny\" rule and the transfer was refused (CVE-2026-70463)"
+fi
+if test "`cat "$spaceddir/f1"`" != "$DATA"; then
+    test_fail "transfer to spaced module was allowed but delivered the wrong content"
+fi
+
+echo "a leading comma in \"auth users\" splits on commas alone"
+exit 0
Description: CVE-2026-53784 - confine the daemon module-root chdir to the served module
 A daemon module configured with "use chroot = no" changes directory into the
 operator-configured module path (module_chdir) with a plain chdir() that
 follows symlinks at every path component. If any component of that path is a
 symlink planted by an untrusted local user (for example an admin path such as
 /home/$user/share, or any /srv-style path under a non-root-owned parent), the
 planted symlink redirects the daemon's CWD out of the configured module
 before any transfer begins, letting a client read from and write to files
 outside the served module (a symlink-race / TOCTOU module escape).
 .
 The upstream fix (defd7110) relies on a large dirfd-stack secure-resolver
 subsystem (util1.c, the *_atfd wrappers, owner_walk_parent,
 secure_relative_open, PID_* macros) that does not exist in the 3.2.3 target
 tree. This is a scoped, target-native equivalent: for a non-chrooted daemon
 module the absolute module path is resolved one component at a time, refusing
 any symlink component not owned by uid 0 or our euid (the trusted operator
 identity) and descending with O_NOFOLLOW so a component cannot be swapped for
 a symlink between the ownership check and the descent, then the daemon
 fchdir()s into the safely-resolved directory. A symlink owned by a trusted
 uid is still followed, so legitimate operator-created links keep working. A
 chrooted module is left on its existing chdir path because chroot() has
 already confined its outer path.
 .
 A regression test (testsuite/daemon-module-chdir-symlink.test) is added,
 translating the upstream daemon-module-chdir-symlink threat model into the
 project's shell test idiom.
Origin: backport, https://github.com/RsyncProject/rsync/commit/defd7110ac9bd60529b3d39cfd5cafe8cc5716de
Author: Patch Platoon <security@example.com>
Last-Update: 2026-08-14
